  "finding_class": "metadata-only",
  "rationale": "Both answers are valid, but the national split is materially overstated: Malaysian Malay officially recognizes both \"jangkitan\" and \"infeksi\" for infection, with \"jangkitan\" a general/common term and \"infeksi\" also established in medical usage. The relation should not be presented as an unqualified different-common-word contrast, and the missing false-friend contrast should remain empty or explicitly state that no false friend exists.",
  "claim_reviews": [
    {
      "subject": "National split and overlap",
      "conclusion": "The split is overstated. Malaysian Malay accepts \"infeksi\" as well as \"jangkitan\"; DBP defines infeksi as infection and lists both as translations of English \"infection\".",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/cari1?keyword=infeksi",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=371752&keyword=penyerangan",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=206468&keyword=jangkitan"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "English cue and sense",
      "conclusion": "The cue \"infection\" and sense \"infection caused by germs\" are substantively aligned with the Indonesian and Malaysian definitions. The wording is slightly simplified but not misleading.",
      "material_risk": false,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemendikdasmen.go.id/entri/infeksi",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=206468&keyword=jangkitan"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Both answers",
      "conclusion": "\"Infeksi\" is a valid Indonesian answer, and \"jangkitan\" is a valid Malaysian Malay answer. However, \"infeksi\" is also a valid Malaysian medical/technical equivalent, so the answers express a usage preference rather than a strict national lexical division.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://kbbi.kemendikdasmen.go.id/entri/infeksi",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/cari1?keyword=infeksi",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=54140&keyword=infection"
      ]
    },
    {
      "subject": "Final relation and false-friend contrast",
      "conclusion": "The relation \"different-common-word\" is too categorical; \"shared-synonym-preference\" or an equivalent overlap-aware relation is more accurate. There is no false-friend contrast because \"infeksi\" and \"jangkitan\" share the intended infection sense.",
      "material_risk": true,
      "evidence_refs": [
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=371752&keyword=penyerangan",
        "https://prpm.dbp.gov.my/Cari1?d=54140&keyword=infection"
      ]
    }
  ]
